<!--
  Supervision - one deadline an application feeds, and what happens when it is missed.

  An application that must keep making progress (a control loop, a task that polls a bus) feeds a
  checkpoint on every pass. If a feed does not come in time, the checkpoint expires, a hook runs
  once, and the node can say that something it supervises has stopped. A node that feeds a
  hardware watchdog feeds it only while every checkpoint is armed, so a hung one stops the feed.
  The design is `docs/observation-plane.md` M3.

  Division of labour with the host (the machine owns the states, the host owns the clock):
    - Whether a deadline has passed is a comparison of three numbers over the monotonic time
      source (now, the last feed, the timeout), and it is the host's: the machine never reads a
      clock and holds no timer. The host checks on its own cadence and raises `deadline.expired`.
    - What the expiry hook does is the application's. The machine says WHEN it runs (once per
      expiry) and that nothing stops it while it runs.
    - Whether a checkpoint that expired comes back on its own is the build's policy
      `supervision-after-expiry`. A policy is a value and a machine cannot read one, so the host
      raises `supervision.rearm` when the policy is `rearm` and a feed arrives in `Expired`; with
      `latch` it does not, and the checkpoint stays expired until it is stopped and started.

  The states:
    Disarmed - not supervised. No deadline is running.
    Armed    - supervised. Every feed starts the deadline again.
    Expiring - the deadline passed and the expiry hook is running. A stop request is not taken
               while it runs: the hook finishes, then the checkpoint is Expired.
    Expired  - the hook has run. A feed is ignored here, so a late feed does not hide a missed
               deadline.

  Engine-free: every effect is a no-arg `<sce:action>` host-trait call, so the generated
  `SupervisionPolicy<A>` carries no `IScriptEngine` and compiles `no_std`.

  Host actions (the generated `SupervisionActions` trait):
    arm_deadline      - entered Armed: start the deadline from the current feed.
    cancel_deadline   - entered Disarmed: stop checking.
    run_expiry_hook   - entered Expiring: run the application's hook, then raise `hook.done`.

  Events fed by the host:
    supervision.start    - begin supervising.
    supervision.feed     - the application made progress.
    supervision.stop     - stop supervising.
    deadline.expired     - the host found the deadline passed.
    hook.done            - the expiry hook returned.
    supervision.rearm    - a checkpoint that expired is to be supervised again.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       xmlns:sce="http://sce.dev/ext"
       xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:schemaLocation="http://sce.dev/ext sce-forge.xsd"
       sce:kind="statechart"
       name="supervision"
       version="1.0"
       initial="Disarmed">

  <state id="Disarmed">
    <onentry>
      <sce:action name="cancel_deadline" effect="duty"/>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="supervision.start" target="Armed"/>
  </state>

  <state id="Armed">
    <onentry>
      <sce:action name="arm_deadline" effect="duty"/>
    </onentry>
    <!-- An external self-transition: leaving and entering Armed again runs arm_deadline, which
         is how a feed starts the deadline over. -->
    <transition event="supervision.feed" target="Armed"/>
    <transition event="deadline.expired" target="Expiring"/>
    <transition event="supervision.stop" target="Disarmed"/>
  </state>

  <!-- No transition on supervision.stop: a checkpoint does not stop while its expiry hook runs. -->
  <state id="Expiring">
    <onentry>
      <sce:action name="run_expiry_hook" effect="duty"/>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="hook.done" target="Expired"/>
  </state>

  <state id="Expired">
    <transition event="supervision.rearm" target="Armed"/>
    <transition event="supervision.stop" target="Disarmed"/>
  </state>

</scxml>
